          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contributions to Electrochemistry</strong><br>\nFaraday laid the groundwork for electrochemistry by establishing the principles of electrolysis. He introduced terms such as electrode, anode, cathode, electrolyte, and ion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Faraday's Law of Electrolysis</strong><br>\nHe formulated Faraday's laws of electrolysis which quantitatively describe the relation between the amount of substance affected by electrolysis and the quantity of electricity used.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Invention of the Faraday Cage</strong><br>\nFaraday invented the Faraday cage, which shields its contents from static and non-static electric fields. This principle is applied in various modern technologies to protect sensitive equipment.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
